# cat-toto — Project Overview

## What This Project Is

`cat-toto` is a faithful CLI replica of the GNU Core Utilities `cat`
(concatenate) command. It reads files sequentially and writes their contents
to standard output. If no file operands are given, or `-` is used as an
operand, it reads from standard input.

On a **plain copy** (no flags), output is a raw `read(2)`/`write(2)` loop
in 64 KiB chunks — maximum throughput with no processing overhead.
When **any formatting flag** is active, a separate formatted path runs a
2 KiB read → transform → write loop that handles line numbers, special
character display, and blank-line squeezing.

## Binary Name

The compiled binary must always be named **`cat-toto`**. Never rename it.
On Windows, MinGW may also emit `cat-toto.exe`; that is the same program.

## Platforms

| Platform | Build environment | Notes |
|----------|-------------------|-------|
| **Linux** | `gcc`/`clang` + GNU Make | Full POSIX path: `sigaction(SIGPIPE, SIG_IGN)`, `open(2)`, `read(2)`, `write(2)` |
| **Windows** | **MSYS2 UCRT64** (recommended) | Native Windows `.exe`; `install_sigpipe_ignore()` is a no-op under `_WIN32` |
| **Git Bash** | Works if `/c/msys64/ucrt64/bin` is **before** Anaconda on `PATH` | Same binary as UCRT64; see `toolchain-structure.mdc` |

Binaries are **not portable** across OSes — build separately on Linux and
on Windows.

## Supported Flags

| Flag | Long form | Meaning |
|------|-----------|---------|
| `-n` | `--number` | Number all output lines |
| `-b` | `--number-nonblank` | Number non-empty lines only (overrides `-n`) |
| `-E` | `--show-ends` | Show `$` at end of each line |
| `-T` | `--show-tabs` | Show TAB as `^I` |
| `-s` | `--squeeze-blank` | Suppress repeated adjacent blank lines |
| `-v` | `--show-nonprinting` | Show non-printing chars using `^` and `M-` notation |
| `-A` | `--show-all` | Equivalent to `-vET` |
| `-e` | *(none)* | Equivalent to `-vE` |
| `-t` | *(none)* | Equivalent to `-vT` |
| `-u` | *(none)* | Ignored (POSIX unbuffered output) |
| | `--help` | Print help and exit 0 |
| | `--version` | Print version and exit 0 |

**Do not add:**

- Interactive or TUI modes
- Input from stdin used as flag data (only as file content)
- Logging or debug output in normal operation
- Flags not listed in the table above
- Any feature that would break GNU `cat` behavioural compatibility

## Behavioural Contract

| Invocation | Behaviour |
|---|---|
| `cat-toto file.txt` | Writes `file.txt` to stdout |
| `cat-toto f1.txt f2.txt` | Concatenates and writes both files in order |
| `cat-toto` | Reads from stdin until EOF (Ctrl+D) |
| `cat-toto -` | Explicit stdin operand; same as above |
| `cat-toto f1.txt - f2.txt` | Writes f1, then stdin, then f2 |
| `cat-toto -n file.txt` | Writes with all lines numbered from 1 |
| `cat-toto -b file.txt` | Numbers non-blank lines only |
| `cat-toto -E file.txt` | Appends `$` before each newline |
| `cat-toto -T file.txt` | Shows TAB characters as `^I` |
| `cat-toto -s file.txt` | Collapses runs of blank lines to one |
| `cat-toto -v file.txt` | Shows non-printing chars as `^X` / `M-X` |
| `cat-toto -A file.txt` | Equivalent to `-v -E -T` |
| `cat-toto --version` | Prints `cat-toto <version>`, exits 0 |
| `cat-toto --help` | Prints usage on stdout, exits 0 |
| `cat-toto nonexistent.txt` | Prints error to stderr, exits 1 |
| `cat-toto f1.txt bad.txt f2.txt` | Writes f1, emits error for bad, writes f2, exits 1 |
| `cat-toto file.txt \| head -n5` | Writes 5 lines then exits 0 on broken pipe |

Line numbers are **continuous across multiple file operands** (GNU `cat`
behaviour): the counter does not reset between files.

## Exit Codes

| Code | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `0` | All files processed successfully; `--help`; `--version`; broken pipe (`EPIPE` on `write`) |
| `1` | Any error: file not found, permission denied, write failure, bad flag, signal setup failure |
| `130` | Ctrl+C / `SIGINT` (`128 + 2`) |

